\f0\fs24 \cf0 Summative Report\
The regression model predicts a company\'92s sales, so the target variable is Sales (in millions of dollars), and the model inputs are the amounts spent on TV, radio and social media advertising plus the type of influencer used. To clean the data, I removed 26 rows with missing values and also one-hot encoded the influencer type. I then used multiple linear regression to train the model. The dataset was split into a training and test dataset with the training set having 80% of the rows. The remaining 20% of the rows were used to test the model predictions. The model was very accurate. It had an R-Squared of 0.999, which means it explains 99.9% of the variation in Sales. It also had a Root Mean Squared Error of about 2.88, which means predictions were usually off by less than $3 million. This is small error because the average sales were about $194 million. TV advertising had by far the biggest impact because every $1 million spent on TV was correlated to about $3.56 million more in sales. Radio, social media and influencer type had coefficients close to zero so they had little effect on Sales. Radio looked important in the correlation heatmap, but then had a small coefficient. This could be because companies that spend more on TV also spend more on radio, so TV is what is responsible for the increase in sales. The residual plot showed small errors which were random. This supports using a linear model for this data. In conclusion, a company in this dataset should focus its marketing budget on TV, since it gives the largest and most significant increase in sales. One limitation is that this regression analysis only shows that TV spending and sales are correlated, but not that one causes the other.\
